    native_zero_arg_method_receiver_not_marshalled_2026-07-19, 1, "2026-11-10 re-scope (fable fix lane, work/p2-native-zero-arg-receiver): the 50.mir premise does not hold. New spec test/01_unit/compiler/50.mir/zero_arg_method_receiver_operand_spec.spl lowers five zero-arg shapes through the pure-Simple HIR->MIR API (struct method on a local, trait method on a local, imported-module class method, chained self.mk().c_ok(), and the engine shape batch.p_ok() on a me-method parameter) and asserts the lowered Call carries exactly one operand that is the receiver (struct value / param / previous call result) and the callee signature declares one receiver param. Red->green: first draft 1/4 green only because it looked for a NAMED receiver local; MIR binds val b = B(..) to the Aggregate dest with no name and spills a struct param through Alloc/Store/GetField->Aggregate/Load, but args=1 in every shape. Final spec 5/5 green on UNMODIFIED source. Mutation proof: dropping the receiver push in lower_receiver_and_args (mir_lowering_stmts.spl) turns 4/5 red (args=0); chained call uses a separate path and still carries its receiver. No lowering change made - adding a receiver in MIR would double it. Remaining scope is below MIR: capture Cranelift IR/disassembly of the exact engine.spl artifact; native gates check-native-consecutive-zero-arg-receiver (check-cranelift-aot-aggregates.shs) and the cross-module zero-arg receiver provider in check-bootstrap-portability.shs already fence the backend. engine.spl inlined workaround (~line 1970) stays until that evidence lands."

